  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 210 PM CDT Mon Aug 3 2026\n\n19Z water vapor imagery showed an upper high over AZ and NM with \nridging extending into the Upper Midwest. An upper low was noted \nover northern Manitoba with a shortwave rotating through the \nnorthern Rockies. Surface obs had low pressure along the lee of the \ncentral Rockies with general high pressure along the MS river \nvalley. This favored continued southerly low level flow. \n\nIn the short term, energy moving across the northern plains is \nexpected to flatten the ridge and allow for some shortwave energy to \nimpact the forecast area through the middle of this week. Models \nshow the better dynamics and chances for precip to be late Tuesday \nnight and into Wednesday morning, then again Thursday. There may be \nsome risk for severe storms Wednesday depending on how the boundary \nlayer destabilizes along a surface boundary over east central KS. \nBut better flow is expected to increase bulk shear and potential for \norganized updrafts. This will be something to keep an eye on. The \nlarger spreads in the short term are for highs Wednesday which will \nbe dependent on how fast the front pushes south along with cloud \ncover and precip. Started highs on Wednesday with the blend and then \ntightened the gradient by cooling highs across the north and raising \nhighs for the southern counties. Have kept some slight chance POPs \nacross north central KS for Tuesday morning as models show some \nconvergence along a sagging boundary. Although the low level jet is \nprogged to veer and weaken as this boundary moves towards Republic \ncounty. So the slight chance POPs look appropriate for now. \n\nBy Friday the ensembles are in reasonable agreement across the \ncentral plains and show the upper ridge redeveloping over the \ncentral Rockies. This is expected to push the mean westerlies back \nto the north of the forecast area. This pattern would favor dry \nweather with temps trending warmer through the weekend and the NBM \nshows this scenario with typical spreads for days four through \neight. One thing to note is the operational solutions are coming in \nmuch warmer for highs Sunday and Monday as 925MB temps warm to \naround 33C. Adiabatically mixing these kinds of temps to the surface \nwould suggest highs more likely around 100 degrees while the NBM has \nkept highs in the middle 90s. This would be closer to the twenty \nfifth percentile of the 00Z ensembles while the seventy fifth \npercentile has highs in the low triple digits. So there is some room \nfor highs to trend hotter by the end of the forecast with heat \nimpacts becoming a greater concern.",
